<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">South West Africa</h1><p class="st-meta">1915 CE – 1990 CE · capital Windhoek</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Namibia under South African rule, a League of Nations mandate turned into a contested apartheid territory until independence in 1990.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>South African forces conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> in 1915. In 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> gave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">South Africa</a> a class C mandate to administer the territory, which it governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a> as if it were a fifth province.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Land was handed to white farmers, while Africans were confined to reserves and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovambo-people">Ovambo</a> men supplied contract labour to mines and farms. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bondelswarts">Bondelswarts</a> rising of 1922 was crushed with aircraft. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apartheid">Apartheid</a> laws were extended after 1948, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odendaal-plan">Odendaal Plan</a> of 1964 set up ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homelands</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> revoked the mandate in 1966, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> declared the occupation illegal in 1971.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> waged an armed struggle from 1966, linked to the war in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a>. After the 1988 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york-accords">New York Accords</a>, UN-supervised elections were held, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> became independent on 21 March 1990 under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-nujoma">Sam Nujoma</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/south-west-africa.jpg" alt="South West Africa" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Parliament of South Africa (Vector graphics image by Denelson83) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Africa_(1928%E2%80%931994,_dark_colors).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1915 CE – 1990 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Windhoek</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Afrikaans, English, German, Oshiwambo, Otjiherero, Khoekhoe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/namibia">Namibia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Suidwes-Afrika, SWA</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1952&amp;focus=south-west-africa" aria-label="South West Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5137 3011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where South West Africa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5457 3267L5428 3067M5457 3267L5434 3101M5457 3267L5456 3293M5457 3267L5457 3267M5457 3267L5746 3380M5457 3267L5746 3380"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5428 3067h0M5434 3101h0M5456 3293h0M5457 3267h0M5746 3380h0M5746 3380h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 3267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 3267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Windhoek. <a href="/atlas?year=1952&amp;focus=south-west-africa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
